#232 -- hold the console-log descriptor open instead of re-acquiring one
per line, and stop discarding the results.

Sys_DebugLog (the -condebug sink, reached from Con_Printf/Con_DPrintf)
opened a FRESH fd for EVERY console line and checked none of the three
syscalls:

    fd = open(file, O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_APPEND, 0666);
    write(fd, data, strlen(data));
    close(fd);

So it needs a free descriptor slot at every single Con_Printf for the
whole run, and a process that runs low on descriptors later simply
stops logging from that point on -- silently, with write(-1, ...) and
close(-1) failing unremarked.

That is not hypothetical here. On Thylacine a hardware-GL client holds
one 9P fid per live BO mapping (the #198 four-table chain), so the
virgl arm is precisely the one that runs low. Measured on thyla-pi:
after deleting the log and proving it absent, a 969-frame timedemo left
it holding exactly 47 bytes -- "Added packfile /quake/id1/pak0.pak (339
files)\n", one line written at Con_Init -- and nothing else, while the
SAME Con_Printf calls kept reaching stdout via Sys_Printf for the whole
27-second run. The identical binary with driver=llvmpipe logs normally,
which is what isolates it to the descriptor budget rather than the
message path.

The failure was invisible from both ends: the engine reported success
it never checked, and any reader of the log (here a bench harness
polling for the timedemo's fps line) sees a healthy run as an infinite
hang.

Acquiring the descriptor once, on first use, takes it early -- before
the renderer's allocations can crowd the table -- and keeps it for the
process lifetime. The path is still honoured: `gamedir` can change it,
so a differing path closes and re-opens, which is what upstream's
per-call open gave for free. A failed open or write now says so once on
stderr and then stays quiet, because a per-line complaint on a
descriptor-starved process would be its own storm.

Not sent upstream: the descriptor-per-line pattern is harmless on a
host with a roomy fd table, which is every platform upstream targets.

--- a/common/sys_unix.c	2026-08-13 12:28:22
+++ b/common/sys_unix.c	2026-08-13 12:28:40
@@ -274,14 +274,50 @@
 {
     va_list argptr;
     static char data[MAX_PRINTMSG];
-    int fd;
+    static char cur[1024];
+    static int fd = -1;
+    static int complained = 0;
 
     va_start(argptr, fmt);
     qvsnprintf(data, sizeof(data), fmt, argptr);
     va_end(argptr);
-    fd = open(file, O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_APPEND, 0666);
-    write(fd, data, strlen(data));
-    close(fd);
+
+    /* Hold the descriptor open instead of re-acquiring one per line. The
+       upstream open/write/close acquires a FRESH fd for every console line,
+       so it needs a free descriptor slot at every Con_Printf for the whole
+       run -- and a process that runs low later loses the log from that point
+       on, silently, because none of the three results was ever checked. On
+       Thylacine a hardware-GL client holds a 9P fid per live BO mapping, so
+       it is exactly the arm that runs low: the log stopped dead after its
+       first line while stdout kept flowing, which is what made a healthy
+       27-second timedemo look like a hang to anything reading the log.
+       Acquiring once, early, takes the descriptor before the renderer's
+       allocations can crowd it out. */
+    if (fd >= 0 && strcmp(cur, file) != 0) {
+        /* `gamedir` changes the path; honour that as upstream's per-call
+           open did, rather than pinning the first file forever. */
+        close(fd);
+        fd = -1;
+    }
+    if (fd < 0) {
+        fd = open(file, O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_APPEND, 0666);
+        if (fd < 0) {
+            if (!complained) {
+                complained = 1;
+                fprintf(stderr, "Sys_DebugLog: cannot open %s: %s"
+                        " -- console logging is now OFF\n",
+                        file, strerror(errno));
+            }
+            return;
+        }
+        strncpy(cur, file, sizeof(cur) - 1);
+        cur[sizeof(cur) - 1] = '\0';
+    }
+    if (write(fd, data, strlen(data)) < 0 && !complained) {
+        complained = 1;
+        fprintf(stderr, "Sys_DebugLog: write to %s failed: %s\n",
+                file, strerror(errno));
+    }
 }
 
 #ifndef USE_X86_ASM
